<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS capability declarations, merged into the generated bundle Info.plist by
  the Tauri CLI (no tauri.conf.json key needed — the file's presence is enough).

  WITHOUT THESE KEYS macOS DOES NOT PROMPT AND DENY — IT REMOVES THE API.
  WKWebView refuses to expose `navigator.mediaDevices` at all when the host app
  has no usage description, so the webview reports
  "undefined is not an object (evaluating 'navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia')"
  — which reads like a broken app rather than a missing permission (reported
  live from a Mac, 2026-08-19). Windows' WebView2 has no such requirement, which
  is why this went unnoticed: the mic worked there the whole time.

  Both keys are load-bearing: the mic is Talk to the DM (dmSpeech.ts), and the
  camera is the table-board read (tableCamera.ts) — same failure, same fix.

  The strings are what the user reads in the macOS permission dialog, so they
  name the FEATURE, not the API.

  No entitlements file accompanies this: entitlements gate capabilities under
  the hardened runtime, which only applies to Apple-signed builds, and these
  releases ship unsigned (see release.yml's quarantine note). If this app is
  ever signed and notarized, it also needs com.apple.security.device.audio-input
  and com.apple.security.device.camera in an Entitlements.plist.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Tavern Sheet uses the microphone so you can talk to the DM out loud instead of typing.</string>
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Tavern Sheet uses the camera so the DM can read your physical battle map.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
